1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
How many gates does a function need? The question has no single answer until you say what a gate is and what you are allowed to start with.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Take one function: A times B, or C. In two-input NAND gates with only A, B, C available it costs three.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Hand it the complements for free and the same function costs two: the inverter disappears into the input.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Every number on this page is computed by the page, by exhaustive search over small circuits, and then re-checked by simulating the circuit on every row.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Four settings fix the answer: which gate type, how many inputs a gate may have, which signals are free, and whether a signal may be reused.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Two-input NAND only. A three-input NAND is not one gate: it is three two-input gates.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Only A, B, C available, or A, B, C and their complements? One gate buys three functions in the first case and nine in the second.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Reuse allowed: a signal computed once may feed several gates. That is what makes two outputs cheaper together than apart.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:05,000
One gate computes not of a product, or not of a sum. Nothing else. That is the whole of what a single gate can do.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
With only x and y available, three functions of two variables are one gate away. AND is not one of them; it takes two.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The exhaustive search reports every depth. Of the sixteen functions of two variables, one needs five NAND gates and only one does.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The one that costs five is exclusive-OR&rsquo;s complement. Its partner, exclusive-OR, costs four.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Here is every function of two variables against four cost models: NAND or NOR, with or without the complements supplied.

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Read the AND row: two NAND gates without complements, and still two with them. NOR gives three, or one.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Read the XOR row: four NAND gates without complements, three with. The folklore four assumes you were not given the complements.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
NAND and NOR swap under duality, and so do AND and OR with XOR and its complement. The counts are not the same function of the row.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Exclusive-OR is the standard worked example, because its two constructions differ by exactly one gate and both are in use.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Four gates: the first makes not-AB, then two gates push A and B against it, and the fourth combines them.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Three gates: not-A-B, A-not-B, then one gate to combine. The saving is the two inverters the first circuit had to build.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So the honest statement is: four gates when you must build the complements, three when they arrive built.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
When the function arrives as a sum of products, the NAND-NAND pattern gives a count directly: one gate per product term, plus one to combine.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Two products of two literals: three gates. A B or C D costs three two-input NAND gates, and that is checked on all sixteen rows.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Three products: the final gate now has three inputs, so it is three gates, not one. Six in total.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
This is the first place the minimal sum of products and the minimal gate count come apart, and the next scene is the second.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A full adder has two outputs. Built separately they cost eight gates and six; built together, nine.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The shared part is the exclusive-OR of the first two inputs, and the not-AB that the four-gate exclusive-OR already built.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Nine gates for the pair, and every one of the eight input rows is simulated here to check both outputs at once.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Nine is a construction this page verifies, not a proved minimum: proving it would need a search over eight-gate circuits in three variables.

29
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
GATE CSE 2009, one mark: the minimum number of gates for AB plus C, using only two-input NOR gates. The key is three.

30
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The trick is to distribute: AB plus C is also A plus C, times B plus C. A product of two sums is what a NOR-NOR circuit builds.

31
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Three gates: one for each sum, inverted, then one to combine. No complements of the inputs are needed anywhere.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And two gates cannot do it. The page searches every two-gate NOR circuit in three variables and finds none that works.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,000
GATE CSE 2017, Set 1: a Karnaugh map, some don&rsquo;t cares, complements available, two-input NOR gates only. The key is one.

34
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Spend the don&rsquo;t cares and the whole map collapses to two literals: not-a AND c.

35
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
not-a AND c is the negation of a OR not-c. That is a single NOR gate, because not-c is supplied.

36
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The map did the work and the complements clause did the rest. Without the complements the same two literals would cost two gates.

37
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
GATE CSE 2019: a four-variable function, sum of minterms zero, two, five, seven, eight, ten, thirteen, fifteen. Two-input NOR gates only.

38
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
The map gives B XNOR D: only two variables survive out of four. The other two never matter.

39
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Three NOR gates: B-not-D inverted, not-B-D inverted, then one gate to combine. The answer is three.

40
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
The folklore table says four for exclusive-NOR in NOR. It says four because it assumes the complements are not supplied.

41
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Step one: read the clause. Gate type, fan-in limit, complements available, sharing allowed.

42
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Step two: simplify, and check whether the result is already one gate. If it is the negation of a product or a sum, stop and answer one.

43
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Step three: match the form. A sum of products wants NAND-NAND; a product of sums wants NOR-NOR.

44
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Step four: count, expand the wide gates, and simulate. A count you did not check is a guess.

45
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
A gate count is a property of a cost model, not of a function. State the model before you state the number.

46
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,000
The numbers worth carrying: NAND gives AND two, OR three, XOR four. NOR gives OR two, AND three, XNOR four.

47
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Two gates save a mark: expand a wide gate before counting it, and reuse a shared signal instead of rebuilding it.

48
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Three previous-year questions on this page, and every one of them turned on a clause rather than on algebra.

